// Deal or Force - who holds a deterrent no neighbour can afford to test (Deterrence.cs).
//
// ─── WHAT THIS FILE IS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The nuclear club of the scenario's opening morning. Each row is a country tag and
// a tier; every other country in the world is an ordinary neighbour. This file is
// WHO ALREADY HAS ONE at the start — acquisition during play is a technology with
// `"effects": { "deterrence": 1 }`, not a row here.
//
// ─── WHAT THE TIERS MEAN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Tier 1  Mutual assured destruction: two tier-1 countries do not fight each other
//         directly (the war judgement prices it out, AiMutualDeterrencePrice).
// Tier 2+ Reserved for mods wanting a second rung — a defence that survives a first
//         strike, say. The code reads "at least 1" everywhere today, so higher tiers
//         make the brinkmanship pressure larger but don't unlock new behaviour yet.
//
// ─── HOW TO CHANGE THE WORLD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// • Earlier era (no bomb): ship this file empty or delete it. The mechanic
//   simply never speaks — no wall, no shadow, no proliferation race.
// • Future era: add rows for whoever your history gave the bomb to.
// • Proliferation in play: add a technology with `effects: deterrence` in
//   `common/technologies/` — the AI reaches for it when a rival has one
//   (TechnologyCore.Choose, the proliferation race).
// • Adjust the wall's price: `AiMutualDeterrencePrice` in `common/defines/`.
//   Default 6 — the order of a whole war's price list. Zero and the wall is gone.
// • Switch everything off: `MutualDeterrenceEnabled` to false in defines.
//
// The club of 2024, as history left it.
{
  "holders": {
    "RUS": 1,
    "USA": 1,
    "CHN": 1,
    "FRA": 1,
    "GBR": 1,
    "IND": 1,
    "PAK": 1,
    "PRK": 1,
    "ISR": 1
  }
}
